$facet runs multiple sub-pipelines on the same input stream and returns one document with one key per pipeline.
db.products.aggregate([
{ $facet: {
by_category: [
{ $group: { _id: "$category", count: { $sum: 1 } } },
{ $sort: { count: -1 } }
],
price_buckets: [
{ $bucket: { groupBy: "$price", boundaries: [0, 10, 50, 100, 500], default: "500+" } }
],
top_10: [
{ $sort: { sales: -1 } },
{ $limit: 10 },
{ $project: { name: 1, sales: 1 } }
]
}}
])
Returns one document:
{
"by_category": [{ "_id": "books", "count": 50 }, ...],
"price_buckets": [{ "_id": 0, "count": 12 }, ...],
"top_10": [{ "name": "...", "sales": 999 }, ...]
}
Perfect for dashboard endpoints that need many statistics from one collection in one round-trip.

{ $bucket: {
groupBy: "$age",
boundaries: [0, 18, 30, 45, 60, 120],
default: "out_of_range",
output: {
count: { $sum: 1 },
avg_age: { $avg: "$age" }
}
}}
Documents get assigned to the bucket whose range contains the groupBy value ([boundary[i], boundary[i+1]) — half-open). Documents outside all ranges go to default.

{ $bucketAuto: {
groupBy: "$price",
buckets: 5,
output: { count: { $sum: 1 } }
}}
Server chooses boundaries so each bucket holds roughly the same number of documents — handy when distribution is unknown.

$merge writes pipeline output into a target collection with upsert / merge semantics. Use for incremental refresh of pre-computed reports.
db.orders.aggregate([
{ $match: { status: "paid" } },
{ $group: {
_id: { $dateTrunc: { date: "$at", unit: "day" } },
revenue: { $sum: "$total" },
orders: { $sum: 1 }
}},
{ $merge: {
into: "daily_revenue",
on: "_id", // unique key
whenMatched: "merge", // merge | replace | keepExisting | fail | pipeline
whenNotMatched: "insert" // insert | discard | fail
}}
])
Schedule the pipeline via a cron job / Atlas Trigger / change-stream-driven Lambda — every run brings the daily summary up to date without rebuilding from scratch.

Window functions compute values over a sliding window of related rows without collapsing them (unlike $group).
db.sales.insertMany([
{ day: "2026-05-01", region: "APAC", revenue: 100 },
{ day: "2026-05-02", region: "APAC", revenue: 120 },
{ day: "2026-05-03", region: "APAC", revenue: 150 },
{ day: "2026-05-01", region: "EMEA", revenue: 80 },
{ day: "2026-05-02", region: "EMEA", revenue: 90 },
])
db.sales.aggregate([
{ $setWindowFields: {
partitionBy: "$region", // separate window per region
sortBy: { day: 1 },
output: {
running_total: { $sum: "$revenue", window: { documents: ["unbounded", "current"] } },
moving_avg_3d: { $avg: "$revenue", window: { documents: [-2, 0] } },
pct_of_total: { $sum: "$revenue", window: { documents: ["unbounded", "unbounded"] } }
}
}}
])
Window types:
documents: [-2, 0] — last 2 rows + current.
documents: ["unbounded", "current"] — all rows up to and including current.
range: [-86400, 0], unit: "second" — time-based window.

Time-series data often has gaps (no rows for days with zero events). $densify inserts placeholder documents at regular intervals; $fill fills missing field values from neighbours or with constants.
db.sales.aggregate([
{ $densify: {
field: "day",
range: { step: 1, unit: "day", bounds: ["2026-05-01", "2026-05-07"] },
partitionByFields: ["region"]
}},
{ $fill: {
output: {
revenue: { value: 0 }
},
sortBy: { day: 1 },
partitionByFields: ["region"]
}}
])
Charts that need a complete date axis (line chart with zero-revenue gaps shown as zero, not skipped) use this.

{ $fill: {
output: { revenue: { method: "linear" } }, // linear interpolation
sortBy: { day: 1 },
partitionByFields: ["region"]
}}
Methods: value: X, method: "linear", method: "locf" (last observation carried forward).

$expr lets aggregation operators appear inside a query filter:
{ $match: {
$expr: { $gt: ["$total", { $multiply: [10, { $size: "$items" }] }] }
}}
Cross-field predicates that find can't express directly — without leaving the aggregation framework.
Also useful inside $lookup pipeline form:
{ $lookup: {
from: "comments",
let: { post_id: "$_id" },
pipeline: [
{ $match: { $expr: { $eq: ["$post", "$$post_id"] } } }
],
as: "comments"
}}

Last resort when no operator combination expresses the logic:
{ $project: {
custom: {
$function: {
body: function (a, b) { return a * 2 + Math.log(b); },
args: ["$x", "$y"],
lang: "js"
}
}
}}
Limits:
Far slower than native operators.
Disabled by default in some Atlas tiers.
Sandboxed but still a security consideration in shared environments.
Use only when truly necessary. Native operators almost always exist for everyday math.
$accumulator is the equivalent for custom $group reductions — same caveats.

"Per-region daily revenue with running total, ranked vs same day other regions, with zero-fill for missing days":
db.sales.aggregate([
{ $match: { day: { $gte: ISODate("2026-05-01"), $lt: ISODate("2026-06-01") } } },
{ $densify: {
field: "day",
range: { step: 1, unit: "day", bounds: ["2026-05-01", "2026-06-01"] },
partitionByFields: ["region"]
}},
{ $fill: { output: { revenue: { value: 0 } }, sortBy: { day: 1 }, partitionByFields: ["region"] } },
{ $setWindowFields: {
partitionBy: "$region",
sortBy: { day: 1 },
output: {
running: { $sum: "$revenue", window: { documents: ["unbounded", "current"] } },
ma7: { $avg: "$revenue", window: { documents: [-6, 0] } }
}
}},
{ $setWindowFields: {
partitionBy: "$day",
sortBy: { revenue: -1 },
output: {
rank_today: { $rank: {} }
}
}},
{ $sort: { day: 1, rank_today: 1 } },
{ $merge: { into: "region_daily_stats", on: ["region", "day"], whenMatched: "merge", whenNotMatched: "insert" } }
])
This kind of pipeline used to require multiple round-trips, app-side post-processing, or a separate analytics warehouse. Now it's one pipeline.

